mcohan@scripps.edu (Max Cohan) writes:

>1) The vision algorithm should 'weigh' walls a bit higher...several times now 
>   I havn't seen walls that are right next to/in front of me.... it makes it 
>   look like a passage, and I wonder why I can't move into it...then I go aroun
>   d a little and it ends up being a wall


I've seen this a few times, too.  Once, in a normal, roguelike level, where
I stepped onto the doorway to a room, revealing the floor in the line-of-
sight cone, then, without completely entering the room, going back into
an outside passage that wrapped around the edge of the room.  *One* of the
room wall spaces adjacent to this passage and not in the line-of-sight cone
was not displayed, not until I went back and re-entered the room
completely.
Sometimes the line-of-sight algorithm seems to be wrong, or at least
non-intuitive.  Several times I've seen small bits of wall or door
of a room I have not yet visited displayed, and there is only room
wall and rock in between my current position and those bits, i.e.,
no doorways close to the straight path, or maybe on another side of my
current room completely.  On non-roguelike levels that have lots
of open space, like the Gnomish Mines, the effect is more pronounced --
upon stepping out into a main, straight, open passage, a good chunk
of the Mine is suddenly displayed all at once, even distant parts of
side passages that are nowhere close to a line of sight.  Fun,
certainly, but not quite right yet if realism is the goal.

-jim

